hadiths[3]{hadithnumber,text}:
"158","Yunus bin Abd al-Ala al-Sadafi narrated to me, Ibn Wahb told me, Yunus told me, on the authority of Ibn Shihab, on the authority of Ata bin Yazid, he told him, Humran told him that one day Uthman called for ablution, so he mentioned the hadith describing the Prophet’s ablution, may God bless him and grant him peace, and said: “Then he washed His right foot to the ankles three times, and his left foot the same way.” Abu Bakr said: This report indicates that the heels are the two protruding bones on either side of the foot, since if the protruding bone On the back of the foot, the right man would have one heel, not two heels."
"159","Narrated to us Abu Ammar, narrated to us Al-Fadl bin Musa, on the authority of Zayd bin Ziyad, he is Ibn Abi Al-Ja’d, on the authority of Jami’ bin Shaddad, on the authority of Tariq Al-Muharabi, He said: I saw the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, passing through the market of Dhu al-Majaz, wearing a red robe, and he was saying: “O people, say: There is no god but By God, you will succeed.” And a man following him threw stones at him, making his ankles and ankles bloody, and he said: O people, do not obey him, for he A liar, so I said: Who is this? They said: A boy of Banu Abdul Muttalib, so I said: Who is this who follows him and throws stones at him? They said: This is Abd al-Uzza Abu Lahab. Abu Bakr said: This report also indicates that the heel is the bone protruding on both sides of the foot, since the throw, if it comes from Behind the walker it is almost impossible to hit the foot, as the leg prevents the throw from hitting the back of the foot."
"160","It was narrated by Salam ibn Junada, it was narrated by Waki`, on the authority of Zakaria ibn Abi Zaida, Abu al-Qasim al-Jadali narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Nu`man ibn Bashir. H, Harun bin Ishaq narrated to us, Ibn Abi Ghaniyeh narrated to us, on the authority of Zakaria, on the authority of Abu al-Qasim al-Jadali, he said: I heard al-Nu’man bin Bashir, saying: The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, came to us and said: “Strengthen your ranks, three, and by God you will straighten your ranks or That God may make a dispute between your hearts.” He said: “I saw the man whose heel was equal to the heel of his companion, and his knee was equal to his companion’s knee, and his shoulder was On its owner's shoulder. This is a comprehensive hadith statement. Abu Bakr said: This Abu al-Qasim al-Jadali is Husayn ibn al-Harith, from the tribe of Qais. Zakaria ibn Abi Zaida narrated on his authority, Abu Malik Al-Ashja’i, Hajjaj ibn Artat, and Ata’ ibn Al-Sa’ib were counted among the Kufis, and in this report he denied doubt and suspicion. The heel is the protruding bone on the side of the foot, which the person standing during prayer can attach to the heel of the person standing next to him in prayer. Prayer, and knowledge is so encompassing for those who have a mind in it that when the worshipers stand in line, none of them can touch the back of their feet. By the back of someone else's footsteps, and this is not possible, and since it is not possible no rational person would imagine that it is"
